#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-owed — what a session still owes Slack, told to it at the end of its turn instead of remembered.

  cc-owed                          what the session in $PWD owes (one line each; exit 1 when anything is)
  cc-owed <repo> <track>           that track's session · --transcript PATH · --session ID · --json
  cc-owed --check                  stdin = a Stop hook payload -> the Stop decision that says it, else nothing
                                   --with '<json>' carries another hook's decision through, ours first
  cc-owed --ledger [--json]        the durable record: one countable line per debt ever said
  cc-owed --answered               stdin = "<chat> <thread_ts> [<since ISO>]" a line -> ONE JSON object a line,
                                   in that order: {chat, root, since, n, first, chat_unknown, unseen} — did
                                   WE say anything in that thread since then, and an n=0 carrying chat_unknown (the
                                   channel reference did not resolve) or unseen (the span asked about is older than
                                   the tail of our own log) is "cannot tell", not "nobody answered".
                                   cc-secretary's asks rubric reads it; see THE ONE THREAD READ below
  cc-owed selfcheck                fixtures for the scan, the three debts, the cancels and the once-only rule

WHY A HOOK AND NOT A RULE. The owner, 2026-08-31: "you sometimes forget to respond in slack or miss
messages... not labelling questions. this could be a symptom of a too complex system". The only thing
tracking what a session still owed was the session, and something that forgets cannot notice that it
forgot. So the box measures it where it already measures how full a context is — the Stop hook, after
every turn — and says it once. Not a line in a prompt: more rules are the thing being objected to.

THREE DEBTS. The first two are read off the session's OWN transcript (the Stop payload's `transcript_path` is
the handle); the third is read off the board and the outbox:
  a word    someone wrote to this session and the turn is ending with nothing said in that thread. What
            happens today instead: five idle minutes later cc-slack posts a 👍 for it ("handled"), which
            is true and says nothing.
  a label   the newest word in a thread is OUR OWN question, posted without `needs_owner` — so the thread
            is on no list of theirs and nothing else will ever put it there. ❓ is the sender's to declare
            (#36): the box still never infers one from prose, it asks the session that wrote it to decide.
  an owner  the board has this track at `waiting` — cc-board's word for "a PERSON must answer before this
            moves", and the only state that reaches the owner's NEEDS YOU list — and no word of ours has
            reached him in any lane since the row ENTERED that state (the board's log says when, because
            `updated` moves every time anyone writes a note). The lanes are his own #<repo>, the automated one
            beside it, the track's own channel, #alerts and #approvals — a card there is a decision put in front
            of him — and in the four that carry more than this one track a word only counts when it NAMES the
            track or its PR, else the box's news about everything else would answer for it (`owner_chats`).
            2026-09-02: a session explained a blocked command in its own window and waited there, and three
            other rows read as reached off #alerts posts about other repos. The state is READ, never inferred,
            and that is what keeps it
            quiet: a session idle at an empty prompt, and one that answered a terminal question in the
            terminal, are `running`, not `waiting`, so neither can raise it.

THE ONE THREAD READ. `outbox_index()` / `answered()` are the single place that answers "was there a word in
that thread, and was it ours", from cc-slack's own outbox.log (one line per post and per reaction, written
before the API call — so no network read, and only words that are OURS). The owner debt asks it backwards
("did we answer him"); cc-secretary's asks rubric asks it forwards ("was this answered") through `--answered`.
Built twice they would disagree, and a disagreement between those two IS a false "the owner is waiting" —
which is the whole of the 2026-09-02 defect: five such findings in ninety minutes, every one answered in its
thread within two minutes. A REACTION IS A WORD here, because it is one everywhere else in this box: cc-slack
tells sessions to react instead of replying when yes/no is the whole answer, and the transcript half above has
always counted one. The box's OWN 👍 for a session that finished silently is not (`react-auto`) — it says
"handled" and nothing else, and counting it would hide the silence this hook exists to notice. NOTHING IT CANNOT SEE IS REPORTED AS SILENCE — an unresolved channel name, an outbox that
will not read, a thread it has no line for, a span older than the tail the log is read from: each comes back as
"cannot tell", which no rule here escalates on.

WHY THE TRANSCRIPT AND NOT A SECOND STORE. Whose turn it is (🔴/🟠) is computed live inside the daemon from
Slack's own history and never lands in a file — re-deriving it here would mean reading Slack, which is a
second source of truth for the one thing cc-slack owns. The transcript is not one: it is this session's own
record of what it was handed and what it said, and it is the only place that knows whether the session
answered. cc-slack's files are consulted for exactly what they do record — a 🏁, a ✅, a ❓ declaration.

THE THREAD'S NEWEST EVENT DECIDES, and only that: a message of theirs with a word of ours after it is
settled, our question with their answer after it is settled. One debt per thread, never a backlog of them.

THE MARKS ARE READ, NEVER RECOMPUTED. cc-slack owns them and is their one writer; this asks Slack nothing
and writes nothing of its own into ~/.cc/slack. Two files cancel a debt:
  flags.json    a human 🏁'd the thread after that message — closed, nothing is owed in it
  marks.json    `:white_check_mark` the session called the thread done · `:question` somebody already
                declared it needs the owner (a `reply(needs_owner=true)`, or cc-slack's own 🔐 prompt),
                within cc-slack's own 60 s grace of the word in question

SAID ONCE per debt, never while another Stop hook is holding the session, never for a `chat_id="local"`
injection (there is no thread on Slack to answer), and never more than MAX_SAY at a time. Every debt said
appends one JSON line to ~/.cc/state/owed/owed.jsonl — countable with `wc -l`, so a reader never parses
prose — and the per-session record beside it is what makes it once (pruned after 7 days).

NEVER BLOCKS A TURN. Every path here exits 0 and swallows its own errors: `--check` is a filter in the
Stop hook, and `--with` means a fault in this tool cannot cost the session the context hook's message.
Env: CC_OWED_RECORDS · CC_SLACK_DIR · CC_OWED_BOARDS (all three so a test run keeps out of the box's own state).
"""
import calendar, glob, json, os, re, subprocess, sys, time

STATE = os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/state")
RECORDS = os.environ.get("CC_OWED_RECORDS") or os.path.join(STATE, "owed")
SLACK_DIR = os.environ.get("CC_SLACK_DIR") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/slack")
PROJECTS = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
TAIL = 512 * 1024          # many turns of transcript; truncation can only hide an old debt, never invent one
PRUNE_AFTER = 7 * 86400
GRACE = 60                 # cc-slack's DECLARE_GRACE: a declaration covers what its sender posts within a minute of it
MAX_SAY = 3

BOARDS = os.environ.get("CC_OWED_BOARDS") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/boards")
OUTBOX_TAIL = 2 * 1024 * 1024   # the log is append-only and ~1 MB a day; the tail is every thread of the last days
SENT = frozenset(("post", "file", "react"))   # outbox kinds that are a WORD OF OURS. `react-auto` is not one:
# it is the 👍 the box itself puts on a message a session finished silently, which says "handled" and no more.
ANSWERLESS = frozenset(("eyes", "question", "red_circle", "large_orange_circle", "large_yellow_circle",
                        "hammer_and_wrench", "checkered_flag"))   # cc-slack's OUR_ANSWER_SKIP by another name:
# 👀 says only "received", ❓🔴🟠 are the bot's own marks and 🏁 closes a thread. ✅ is NOT here — it says done.
ANSWERLESS_CHAR = {"👀": "eyes", "❓": "question", "🔴": "red_circle", "🟠": "large_orange_circle",
                   "🟡": "large_yellow_circle", "🔧": "hammer_and_wrench", "🛠": "hammer_and_wrench",
                   "🏁": "checkered_flag"}   # THE SAME MARKS, SPELT THE OTHER WAY (cc-slack's own emoji tables)

SENDERS = {"mcp__cc-slack__reply", "mcp__cc-slack__file", "mcp__cc-slack__react"}
CHAN_RX = re.compile(r'<channel\s+([^>]*?)>\n?(.*?)</channel>', re.S)
ATTR_RX = re.compile(r'([a-z_]+)="([^"]*)"')
CHAT_RX = re.compile(r'\b[CDG][A-Z0-9]{6,}\b')      # a Slack channel/DM id on a command line
TS_RX = re.compile(r'\b\d{10}\.\d{6}\b')            # a Slack message ts on one


def load_slack(name):
    """One of cc-slack's own state files, read and never written: {"<key>": <epoch>}."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, name)) as f:
            return {k: float(v) for k, v in json.load(f).items()}
    except Exception:
        return {}


def tail_of(path, nbytes):
    """(the last <=nbytes of a file as whole lines, whether that was the WHOLE file). A truncated first line is
    dropped. The second half is the difference between "nothing there" and "cannot see": truncation is silent, so
    a reader asking about a span older than the tail is not looking at silence, it is looking at what rolled off."""
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        size = f.seek(0, 2)
        start = max(0, size - nbytes)
        f.seek(start)
        buf = f.read()
    if start:
        buf = buf.split(b"\n", 1)[1] if b"\n" in buf else b""
    return [l for l in buf.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n") if l.strip()], not start


def epoch(iso):
    """A transcript entry's ISO timestamp as a UTC epoch (0.0 when it has none)."""
    try:
        return float(calendar.timegm(time.strptime(iso[:19], "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S")))
    except Exception:
        return 0.0


# ---- the one thread read: did WE put a word in there ---------------------------------------------
# Both halves of the 2026-09-02 defect need the same fact and it is small: for a thread, was there a word,
# and was it ours. cc-secretary asks it forwards ("was this answered") through `--answered`; the owner debt
# below asks it backwards ("did we answer him"). One reader, so the two can never disagree — and a
# disagreement between them is exactly a false "the owner is waiting".

_CHANS = {}        # SLACK_DIR -> its channels.json, read once (a test moves SLACK_DIR, so the dir is the key)


def channels():
    """cc-slack's channels.json, or {} when it is not readable — it is unlinked and rewritten on mkchannel, so
    "not there" is a normal moment, not a fault. cc-slack is its one writer; this only ever reads it."""
    if SLACK_DIR not in _CHANS:
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "channels.json")) as f:
                _CHANS[SLACK_DIR] = json.load(f) or {}
        except Exception:
            _CHANS[SLACK_DIR] = {}
    return _CHANS[SLACK_DIR]


def chat_ref(ref):
    """(the id, whether it RESOLVED). A bare C…/D…/G… id is itself; a name resolves through channels.json.
    Anything else is unresolved — `dm`, which is cc-scope's own word for an IM and is no channel's name; a
    channel made since that file was last written; any name at all while it is missing — and it comes back
    False so the caller can say "I cannot tell" instead of "nobody answered". An unresolved name matches no
    key, so it reads n=0, and an n=0 that means unknown is the false alarm wearing the true one's clothes."""
    ref = str(ref or "").strip()
    if re.fullmatch(r"[CDG][A-Z0-9]{6,}", ref):
        return ref, True
    got = channels().get(ref.lstrip("#"))
    return (str(got), True) if got else (ref, False)


def chat_id(ref):
    """The id alone, for the places that only have to name the channel back to a session."""
    return chat_ref(ref)[0]


def reaction_name(e):
    """What a reaction is CALLED, whichever way it was spelt. A session may react with the name or with the
    character, `eyes` or `👀`, and cc-slack forwards whichever it was given and writes that to outbox.log — its
    own handler tests both spellings for exactly this reason. So one spelling here is a hole with a direction:
    a 👀 says "received" and nothing else, and read as a word it makes a question nobody answered unflaggable,
    which is the silence this whole tool exists to notice."""
    e = str(e or "").strip().strip(":").replace("\ufe0f", "").lower()
    return ANSWERLESS_CHAR.get(e, e)


class Outbox(dict):
    """The index, plus WHERE IT BEGINS. `begins` is the epoch of the oldest line the read could see when the log
    was longer than the tail it was read from, and 0.0 when the whole file was read and nothing is hidden. The log
    rolls and the truncation is silent, so a word that has scrolled out of it reads exactly like a word that was
    never posted — while the owner debt asks an UNBOUNDED question, "has anything reached him since this row
    entered waiting". Without this, a row that waited longer than the tail is nagged about a message he already
    has, which is the false alarm this whole tool exists to stop (review of PR #194)."""
    begins = 0.0


def outbox_index(nbytes=OUTBOX_TAIL):
    """Every word this box has SENT, indexed by where it landed: {(chat, thread_ts): [(epoch, text)…]} plus
    {(chat, None): [(epoch, text)…]} for the channel as a whole. The text rides along because a SHARED lane needs
    it: #alerts carries every repo's news, and telling a word about this track from a word about another one is
    reading what it says (see `owner_chats`). cc-slack writes one outbox.log line per post and per
    reaction before it calls the API, so this asks Slack nothing, costs no network read, and sees exactly the
    words that are OURS. A REACTION COUNTS: on their message it IS the answer — cc-slack tells sessions so, and
    the transcript half of this tool has always read one that way — so leaving it out would put the two halves
    in disagreement, which is the false "the owner is waiting" all over again. It is filed under the ts it sits
    on, which for a thread's own root is that thread. Bookkeeping reactions answer nothing and are skipped.
    NO FILE means nothing has ever been posted, which is an honest empty. A file that will not READ raises, and
    every caller here turns that into silence rather than "unanswered" — a failed read must not come back as the
    false "the owner is waiting" this whole change exists to stop. ONLY THE LAST `nbytes` ARE READ, so a word
    older than that is gone: `Outbox.begins` dates the oldest line this read saw, and no caller may report an n=0
    from before it as silence."""
    idx, first = Outbox(), 0.0
    try:
        lines, whole = tail_of(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), nbytes)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return idx
    for l in lines:
        f = l.split("\t", 4)
        if len(f) < 4:
            continue
        at = epoch(f[0])
        if not at:
            continue
        first = first or at             # append-only, so the first line it could date is the oldest it can see
        if f[1] not in SENT:
            continue                    # `react-auto` is the box's own 👍 for a session that said nothing: it
                                        # means "handled" and no more, and counting it would hide that silence
        if f[1] == "react" and reaction_name(f[4] if len(f) > 4 else "") in ANSWERLESS:
            continue
        said = f[4] if len(f) > 4 else ""
        idx.setdefault((f[2], f[3]), []).append((at, said))
        idx.setdefault((f[2], None), []).append((at, said))
    if not whole:
        idx.begins = first or time.time()   # the read started mid-file: everything before this line has rolled
                                            # off, and a tail it could date no line in answers for nothing at all
    return idx


def answered(chat, root, since=0.0, idx=None, naming=None):
    """{"n": how many words of ours, "first": the epoch of the earliest, "chat_unknown": the channel reference
    did not resolve} in that thread at or after `since`. `root=None` asks about the whole channel instead of one
    thread, and `naming` narrows that to the words that mention one of its strings — a whole channel is only ever
    a scope for something (see `owner_chats`), never for a question, which has a thread of its own. SECOND
    RESOLUTION, because that is what outbox.log prints: a word stamped in the same second as the
    question counts as an answer to it. When the reference did not resolve, the id is not ours to trust, so for a
    THREAD we look its root up under every chat in the index — a root is a microsecond stamp, so a match is that
    thread — and either way `chat_unknown` says the n=0 is "cannot tell", not "nobody answered". `unseen` says the
    same about the other blindness: the index's tail begins after `since`, so part of the span asked about had
    rolled off the log before it was read. Building the index here can raise OSError (see `outbox_index`); pass one
    in, or catch it — never report a failed read as 0."""
    idx = outbox_index() if idx is None else idx
    cid, known = chat_ref(chat)
    root = None if root is None else str(root)
    ats = idx.get((cid, root), [])
    if not known and root is not None:
        ats = [a for k, v in idx.items() if k[1] == root for a in v]
    if naming:
        # review of PR #194: a bare substring let an unrelated post in a shared lane clear the debt — track
        # `api` was named by "alpha/rapid-fix done", which contains it inside "rapid". Both ends are bounded, so
        # a longer row's name cannot pay a shorter one's either (`alpha/t9` does not match `alpha/t9x`).
        ats = [(a, t) for a, t in ats
               if any(re.search(r"(?<!\w)" + re.escape(w) + r"(?!\w)", t, re.I) for w in naming)]
    ats = sorted(a for a, _ in ats if a >= (since or 0))
    begins = getattr(idx, "begins", 0.0)      # 0.0: the whole log was read, so it hides nothing from anyone
    # the tail begins at or after `since`, so a word paying this could have gone out inside the part that rolled
    # off. The boundary second counts as unseen: the oldest line kept is stamped in it and outbox.log prints
    # seconds, so the rest of that second is exactly what the read lost.
    return {"n": len(ats), "first": ats[0] if ats else None, "chat_unknown": not known,
            "unseen": bool(begins) and begins >= (since or 0)}


def text_of(message):
    c = (message or {}).get("content")
    if isinstance(c, str):
        return c
    if isinstance(c, list):
        return "\n".join(b.get("text", "") for b in c if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text")
    return ""


def question(text):
    """THE question OUR OWN word ends on, or "". The LAST line only, outside code fences, never a quote of
    theirs: a rhetorical question in the middle of an answer is not an ask. The box's own two forms (a 🔐 prompt,
    a worker's STATUS: BLOCKED) declare themselves in cc-slack and are not this hook's business. Wider than
    cc-slack's asks_owner on purpose — that one decides what the OWNER sees, this one only ever reaches the
    session that wrote the words, and it can say no by ignoring it."""
    t = text or ""
    if t.strip().startswith("🔐") or any(l.lstrip().upper().startswith("STATUS: BLOCKED") for l in t.splitlines()):
        return ""
    parts = t.split("```")
    body = "".join(parts[i] for i in range(0, len(parts), 2))
    lines = [l.strip() for l in body.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    last = lines[-1] if lines else ""
    return last if last.endswith("?") and not last.startswith(">") else ""


def words(name, inp):
    """Every word this tool call says on Slack: (chat, {ts it lands on} or None, text, needs_owner, is a reply).
    None = a post with no thread of its own, which settles anything owed in that channel rather than one thread."""
    inp = inp if isinstance(inp, dict) else {}
    if name in SENDERS:
        chat = str(inp.get("chat_id") or "")
        ids = {str(inp.get(k)) for k in ("thread_ts", "ts") if inp.get(k)}
        if chat:
            return [(chat, ids or None, str(inp.get("text") or ""), bool(inp.get("needs_owner")),
                     name.endswith("reply"))]
    if name == "Bash":                      # `cc-slack post`/`react` from a script does the same job as the tool
        cmd = str(inp.get("command") or "")
        if "cc-slack" in cmd and re.search(r"\b(post|react)\b", cmd):
            ids = set(TS_RX.findall(cmd)) or None
            return [(c, ids, "", False, False) for c in set(CHAT_RX.findall(cmd))]
    return []


def scan(path):
    """The NEWEST event in every Slack thread this transcript touches: {(chat, root): event}. An event is either
    a message handed to this session (`in`) or a word of ours (`out`)."""
    st = {}
    for line in tail_of(path, TAIL)[0]:
        try:
            d = json.loads(line)
        except Exception:
            continue                        # a half-written last line is normal while a session runs
        if not isinstance(d, dict):
            continue
        at, t = epoch(d.get("timestamp") or ""), d.get("type")
        if t == "user":
            for attrs, body in CHAN_RX.findall(text_of(d.get("message") or {})):
                a = dict(ATTR_RX.findall(attrs))
                chat, root = a.get("chat_id") or "", a.get("thread_ts") or a.get("ts") or ""
                if a.get("source") != "cc-slack" or not chat or not root or chat == "local":
                    continue                # an injection on the box has no Slack thread to owe a word to
                if a.get("kind") == "reaction":
                    continue                # their 👍/👎 IS the answer ("act without asking again") — it owes no
                                            # word, and skipping it leaves an older unanswered message the newest
                body, ts = body.strip(), a.get("ts") or root
                if body.startswith("[") and body.endswith("]"):
                    cur = st.get((chat, root))      # cc-slack's own notice ABOUT a message, not a word from them:
                    if cur and cur["kind"] == "in" and cur["ts"] == ts:   # "deleted … — disregard it" settles what
                        del st[(chat, root)]                              # that message owed. (An edit notice carries
                    continue                                              # the new text after the "]" and is a word.)
                st[(chat, root)] = {"kind": "in", "chat": chat, "root": root, "ts": ts,
                                    "who": a.get("user") or "someone", "role": a.get("role") or "",
                                    "chan": a.get("channel") or chat, "text": body, "at": at}
        elif t == "assistant":
            for c in (d.get("message") or {}).get("content") or []:
                if not isinstance(c, dict) or c.get("type") != "tool_use":
                    continue
                for chat, ids, text, needs, is_reply in words(c.get("name") or "", c.get("input")):
                    out = {"kind": "out", "chat": chat, "text": text, "needs": needs, "reply": is_reply, "at": at}
                    if ids is None:         # a word into the channel itself: everything open there has had one
                        for k in [k for k in st if k[0] == chat]:
                            st[k] = carry(st[k], out)
                        continue
                    hit = [k for k in st if k[0] == chat and (k[1] in ids or st[k].get("ts") in ids)]
                    for k in hit:
                        st[k] = carry(st[k], out)
                    if not hit and is_reply:
                        root = str((inp_root(c.get("input")) or ""))
                        if root:            # a thread whose inbound message is older than this transcript's tail
                            st[(chat, root)] = dict(out, root=root)
    return st


def carry(prev, out):
    """Our word takes the thread over — but the channel's name and who is in it belong to the thread."""
    return dict(out, root=prev["root"], chan=prev.get("chan"), who=prev.get("who"))


def inp_root(inp):
    inp = inp if isinstance(inp, dict) else {}
    return inp.get("thread_ts") or inp.get("ts")


def board(repo):
    """That repo's board as cc-board wrote it, or {}. Read only: the board is cc-board's."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(BOARDS, f"{repo}.json")) as f:
            return json.load(f) or {}
    except Exception:
        return {}


def board_row(b, track):
    """That track's row, or None."""
    ts = (b or {}).get("tracks") or {}
    if isinstance(ts, dict):
        row = ts.get(track)
        return row if isinstance(row, dict) else None
    for row in ts if isinstance(ts, list) else []:
        if isinstance(row, dict) and row.get("name") == track:
            return row
    return None


def wait_start(b, track, row):
    """WHEN this row entered `waiting`, off the board's own log — its last `status -> …` line for the track, when
    that line is the one that said waiting. NOT `updated`: cc-board bumps that on every note and set, so a note
    typed after the owner was told would restart the clock and re-fire a debt he has already paid. `updated` is
    the fallback for a row whose log line has been pruned, or that was set by hand without one."""
    for e in reversed((b or {}).get("log") or []):
        if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get("track") != track:
            continue
        msg = str(e.get("msg") or "")
        if msg.startswith("status -> "):
            return epoch(e.get("ts") or "") if msg == "status -> waiting" else epoch((row or {}).get("updated") or "")
    return epoch((row or {}).get("updated") or "")


def owner_chats(repo, track, row=None):
    """Every place a word of ours reaches the owner from this track, each with what a word there has to NAME to
    be about THIS track. Any of them counts — the debt is that he was told NOTHING, not that a particular rung
    was used — and #approvals is one of them, because a card there is how this box asks him for a decision he
    only has to tap (cc-reconcile flips a row to `waiting` and posts one, and nothing else).
    FOUR OF THE FIVE ARE SHARED: his lane #<repo> and the automated lane beside it carry every track in the repo,
    #alerts and #approvals carry every repo on the box. A word in one of them that does not name this track is
    not a word about this track — read channel-wide, all three of ai-ee's waiting rows came back "he was told"
    on 2026-09-02 because #alerts had 122 posts about other things since they started waiting. Naming is how the
    box already writes: a track post says `<repo>/<track>`, and a card or any post that links the work carries
    the PR's own url, which is the row's `pr`. The track's own channel #<repo>--<track> IS the track, so anything
    in it counts. #<repo> is FIRST: when his own lane does not resolve, `owner_debt` is blind and says nothing."""
    # `<repo>/<track>`, not the bare track: it is how this box already writes a track post, it is what this
    # docstring says, and the bare name matched other rows' posts in the shared lanes (review of PR #194).
    names = [w for w in (f"{repo}/{track}", str((row or {}).get("pr") or "").strip()) if w]
    return [(f"#{repo}", names), (f"#{repo}-updates", names), (f"#{repo}--{track}", None),
            ("#alerts", names), ("#approvals", names)]


def owner_debt(repo, track, idx=None):
    """THE THIRD DEBT. The board says this track is `waiting` — cc-board's own word for "a PERSON must answer
    before this moves", and the only state that reaches the owner's NEEDS YOU list — and nothing of ours has
    reached him since the row said so. The state is READ, never inferred, which is what keeps it quiet: a
    session idle at an empty prompt and one that answered a terminal question in the terminal are `running`,
    not `waiting`, so neither can raise this. It dates from when the row ENTERED waiting (`wait_start`), so the
    debt is once per wait, not once per turn and not once per note. It says NOTHING when it cannot see the whole
    span it is asking about: his own lane unnameable, the outbox unreadable, or the log rolled past the moment the
    row entered waiting."""
    if not repo or not track:
        return None
    b = board(repo)
    row = board_row(b, track)
    if not row or row.get("status") != "waiting":
        return None
    since = wait_start(b, track, row)
    try:
        idx = outbox_index() if idx is None else idx
    except OSError:
        return None                                   # the outbox would not read: never claim he was not told
    lanes = [answered(c, None, since, idx, naming) for c, naming in owner_chats(repo, track, row)]
    if lanes[0]["chat_unknown"]:
        return None                                   # his own lane #<repo> does not resolve — we are blind to
                                                      # the one place he reads, so we cannot say he was not told
    if any(a["n"] for a in lanes):
        return None                                   # he was reached, by whichever rung: nothing is owed
    if lanes[0]["unseen"]:
        return None                                   # the log's tail begins after this row entered waiting, so
                                                      # the word that paid it may have scrolled out of the log:
                                                      # an n=0 over a span the read could not see is not silence.
                                                      # The tail is days of it, and a wait older than that is not
                                                      # the case this hook is for (review of PR #194)
    return {"kind": "owner", "chat": chat_id(f"#{repo}"), "root": "", "chan": f"#{repo}", "who": "you",
            "text": str(row.get("title") or f"{repo}/{track}"), "at": since, "repo": repo, "track": track,
            "pr": str(row.get("pr") or "").strip(),      # `told` offers it as the other thing a word can name
            "key": f"owner:{repo}/{track}:{int(since)}"}


def debts(path, marks=None, flags=None, where=None):
    """What is owed right now, newest first. A debt is a thread whose newest event leaves the ball here — or,
    when `where` names this session's (repo, track), a board row that says it is waiting on the owner and no
    word of ours since. Both read the same outbox index, so the two can never disagree about a thread."""
    marks = load_slack("marks.json") if marks is None else marks
    flags = load_slack("flags.json") if flags is None else flags
    out = []
    for (chat, root), e in scan(path).items():
        when = float(e.get("ts") or root or 0) if e["kind"] == "in" else e["at"]
        if flags.get(f"{chat}:{root}", 0) >= when:
            continue                                              # a human 🏁'd it after that: the thread is closed
        q = marks.get(f"{chat}:{root}:question", 0)
        if q and q + GRACE >= when:
            continue                                              # already declared: the ball is theirs, not ours
        if e["kind"] == "in":
            if marks.get(f"{chat}:{root}:white_check_mark", 0) >= when:
                continue                                          # the session called the thread done
            out.append(dict(e, kind="word", key=f"word:{chat}:{e.get('ts') or root}"))
        elif e.get("reply") and not e["needs"] and question(e["text"]):
            out.append(dict(e, kind="label", key=f"label:{chat}:{root}:{int(when)}"))
    o = owner_debt(*(where or (None, None)))
    if o:
        out.append(o)
    out.sort(key=lambda d: -(d["at"] or 0))
    return out


# ---- what the session is told ------------------------------------------------------------------

def age(since):
    """How long it has waited, in the shortest form that is true."""
    d = time.time() - (since or 0)
    if not since or d < 60:
        return "just now"
    for n, unit in ((86400, "d"), (3600, "h"), (60, "m")):
        if d >= n:
            return f"{int(d // n)}{unit}"
    return "just now"


def excerpt(t, n=70):
    s = " ".join((t or "").split())
    return (s[:n - 1] + "…") if len(s) > n else s


def told(d):
    if d["kind"] == "word":
        return (f"OWED — {d['who']} wrote in {d['chan']} {age(float(d.get('ts') or 0))} ago and this turn is ending "
                f"with nothing said in that thread: "
                f"\"{excerpt(d['text'])}\". Answer it now — reply(chat_id=\"{d['chat']}\", thread_ts=\"{d['root']}\", "
                f"ts=\"{d['ts']}\"), or react on that ts when 👍/👎/❌ says all of it. Two words of your own beat the "
                f"👍 the box posts for a session that finishes silently.")
    if d["kind"] == "owner":
        # what it prescribes has to be what `owner_chats` counts, or the session does as it is told and the next
        # one is told the same thing again about a message he already has: his lanes carry every track in the repo
        name = f"{d['repo']}/{d['track']}" + (f" or {d['pr']}" if d.get("pr") else "")
        return (f"HE DOES NOT KNOW — the board has {d['repo']}/{d['track']} at `waiting` since {age(d['at'])} ago, which "
                f"is its word for \"a person has to answer before this moves\", and nothing of ours has gone to him in "
                f"{d['chan']} or any other lane since: \"{excerpt(d['text'])}\". Say it where he reads — "
                f"reply(chat_id=\"{d['chat']}\", needs_owner=true), or a --mention post on {d['chan']} — and NAME the row "
                f"in it ({name}): {d['chan']} carries every track in the repo, so a word that names none of them is not "
                f"a word about this one. If you are not actually waiting on him, take the row off `waiting` instead.")
    return (f"UNLABELLED — your last word in {d.get('chan') or d['chat']} ({age(d['at'])} ago) asks them something without "
            f"needs_owner, so the thread is on no list of theirs and nothing else will put it there: "
            f"\"{excerpt(question(d['text']) or d['text'])}\". If you have STOPPED until they answer, say so — "
            f"reply(chat_id=\"{d['chat']}\", "
            f"thread_ts=\"{d['root']}\", needs_owner=true). If you are not waiting on them, ignore this.")


def line(d):
    return (f"{d['kind']}\t{d.get('chan') or d['chat']}\t{age(float(d.get('ts') or 0) or d['at'])}"
            f"\t{d.get('who') or 'us'}\t{excerpt(question(d['text']) if d['kind'] == 'label' else d['text'], 60)}"
            f"\t{d['chat']}\t{d['root']}")


# ---- the record: once each, and countable ------------------------------------------------------

def ledger(d, m):
    os.makedirs(RECORDS, exist_ok=True)
    row = {"ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), "kind": d["kind"], "chat": d["chat"],
           "root": d["root"], "msg_ts": d.get("ts"), "chan": d.get("chan"), "who": d.get("who"),
           "session_id": m.get("session_id"), "repo": m.get("repo"), "track": m.get("track")}
    with open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "owed.jsonl"), "a") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps(row) + "\n")


def ledger_rows():
    out = []
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "owed.jsonl")) as f:
            for l in f:
                try:
                    out.append(json.loads(l))
                except Exception:
                    continue                       # one bad line never hides the rest
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


def track_of(cwd):
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(cwd, ".cc/track")) as f:
            repo, track = [l.strip() for l in f.read().splitlines()[:2]]
        return repo, track
    except Exception:
        return None, None


def prune():
    now = time.time()
    for old in glob.glob(os.path.join(RECORDS, "*.json")):
        try:
            if now - os.path.getmtime(old) > PRUNE_AFTER:
                os.remove(old)
        except OSError:
            pass


def check(payload):
    """Called by the Stop hook. Never raises: what a session owes must not be able to break it."""
    if payload.get("stop_hook_active"):
        return None                                   # another hook is holding the session; it is not being told twice
    sid, path = payload.get("session_id") or "", payload.get("transcript_path") or ""
    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):
        hits = glob.glob(os.path.join(PROJECTS, "*", sid + ".jsonl")) if sid else []
        path = max(hits, key=os.path.getmtime) if hits else ""
    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):
        return None
    cwd = payload.get("cwd") or ""
    repo, track = track_of(cwd)
    ds = debts(path, where=(repo, track))
    if not ds:
        return None
    p = os.path.join(RECORDS, (sid or os.path.basename(path)[:-6]) + ".json")
    rec = {}
    try:
        with open(p) as f:
            rec = json.load(f) or {}
    except Exception:
        pass
    said = rec.get("told") or {}
    fresh = [d for d in ds if d["key"] not in said][:MAX_SAY]     # said once per debt, not once per turn
    if not fresh:
        return None
    now = time.time()
    for d in fresh:
        said[d["key"]] = now
        ledger(d, {"session_id": sid, "repo": repo, "track": track})
    os.makedirs(RECORDS, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump({"ts": now, "cwd": cwd, "repo": repo, "track": track,
                   "told": {k: v for k, v in said.items() if v >= now - PRUNE_AFTER}}, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    prune()
    return " ".join(told(d) for d in fresh)


def merge(say, carried):
    """One Stop decision out of two hooks' worth of things to say — what is OWED comes first: a session about to
    hand off has to pay its debts before it goes."""
    reasons = [r for r in (say, (json.loads(carried).get("reason") if carried else None)) if r]
    return json.dumps({"decision": "block", "reason": "\n\n".join(reasons)}) if reasons else ""


# ---- finding a session's transcript ------------------------------------------------------------

def transcript_for_cwd(cwd):
    d = os.path.join(PROJECTS, re.sub(r"[/.]", "-", os.path.realpath(cwd)))
    hits = glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.jsonl"))
    return max(hits, key=os.path.getmtime) if hits else None


def resolve(repo=None, track=None, sid=None):
    if sid:
        hits = glob.glob(os.path.join(PROJECTS, "*", sid + ".jsonl"))
        return max(hits, key=os.path.getmtime) if hits else None
    if repo and track:
        return transcript_for_cwd(os.path.expanduser(f"~/.cc/worktrees/{repo}/{track}"))
    return transcript_for_cwd(os.getcwd())


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["selfcheck"]:
        return selfcheck()
    as_json = do_check = do_ledger = do_answered = False
    path = sid = carried = None
    args = []
    while argv:
        a = argv.pop(0)
        if a == "--json":
            as_json = True
        elif a == "--check":
            do_check = True
        elif a == "--ledger":
            do_ledger = True
        elif a == "--answered":
            do_answered = True
        elif a == "--with":
            carried = argv.pop(0) if argv else None
        elif a == "--transcript":
            path = argv.pop(0)
        elif a == "--session":
            sid = argv.pop(0)
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            return 0
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            print(f"cc-owed: unknown option {a}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        else:
            args.append(a)

    if do_check:
        say = None
        try:
            payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
            say = check(payload if isinstance(payload, dict) else {})
        except Exception:
            pass                                      # a debt this tool cannot read is not a session's problem
        try:
            out = merge(say, carried)
        except Exception:
            out = carried or ""                       # whatever the other hook said still goes, unchanged
        if out:
            print(out)
        return 0

    if do_answered:
        try:
            idx = outbox_index()
        except OSError:
            return 0        # say NOTHING rather than "no": a line missing from this reads as unknown, not unanswered
        for l in sys.stdin.read().splitlines():
            if not l.strip():
                continue                  # ONE object per non-blank line, in that order: the caller pairs them up
            f = l.split("\t") if "\t" in l else l.split()
            chat, root, since = (f + ["", "", ""])[0], (f + ["", "", ""])[1], (f + ["", "", ""])[2]
            a = answered(chat, root or None, epoch(since), idx)
            print(json.dumps({"chat": chat, "root": root, "since": since, "n": a["n"],   # the keys the header lists,
                              "first": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(a["first"])) if a["first"] else "",
                              "chat_unknown": a["chat_unknown"], "unseen": a["unseen"]}))  # in the order it lists them
        return 0

    if do_ledger:
        rows = ledger_rows()
        if as_json:
            print(json.dumps(rows, indent=2))
            return 0
        for r in rows:
            print(f"{r.get('ts')}\t{r.get('kind')}\t{(r.get('repo') or '-')}/{(r.get('track') or '-')}"
                  f"\t{r.get('chan') or r.get('chat')}\t{r.get('root')}")
        return 0

    if not path:
        path = resolve(args[0] if args else None, args[1] if len(args) > 1 else None, sid)
    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):
        print("cc-owed: no transcript for that session", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    where = (args[0], args[1]) if len(args) > 1 else track_of(os.getcwd())
    ds = debts(path, where=where)
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(ds, indent=2))
    else:
        for d in ds:
            print(line(d))
    return 1 if ds else 0


# ---- selfcheck ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def selfcheck():
    import tempfile
    global RECORDS, SLACK_DIR
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        print(("ok   " if cond else "FAIL ") + name)
        p, f = p + (1 if cond else 0), f + (0 if cond else 1)

    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cc-owed.")
    RECORDS, SLACK_DIR = os.path.join(tmp, "rec"), os.path.join(tmp, "slack")
    os.makedirs(SLACK_DIR)
    T0 = 1700000000.0
    CHAT, ROOT = "C111AAA22", "1700000000.000100"

    def iso(t):
        return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(t))

    def msg(text="do the thing", ts=ROOT, root=ROOT, chat=CHAT, chan="#one", who="Owner", at=T0 + 1, kind=""):
        tag = (f'<channel source="cc-slack" chat_id="{chat}" thread_ts="{root}" ts="{ts}" user="{who}" '
               f'{f"""kind="{kind}" """ if kind else ""}role="owner" channel="{chan}" target="one">\n{text}\n</channel>')
        return {"type": "user", "timestamp": iso(at), "message": {"role": "user", "content": tag}}

    def use(name, inp, at=T0 + 2):
        return {"type": "assistant", "timestamp": iso(at),
                "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "name": name, "input": inp}]}}

    def rep(text="on it", needs=False, root=ROOT, ts=ROOT, chat=CHAT, at=T0 + 2, **extra):
        inp = {"chat_id": chat, "text": text, "thread_ts": root, "ts": ts}
        if needs:
            inp["needs_owner"] = True
        inp.update(extra)
        return use("mcp__cc-slack__reply", inp, at)

    n = [0]

    def tx(*entries):
        n[0] += 1
        path = os.path.join(tmp, f"t{n[0]}.jsonl")
        with open(path, "w") as fh:
            for e in entries:
                fh.write(json.dumps(e) + "\n")
        return path

    def kinds(path, **kw):
        return [d["kind"] for d in debts(path, **kw)]

    # --- the scan: the thread's newest event, and only that, decides
    ok("a message with no word after it is owed", kinds(tx(msg())) == ["word"])
    ok("a reply in that thread settles it", kinds(tx(msg(), rep())) == [])
    ok("a reply BEFORE it does not — their word is the newer one",
       kinds(tx(rep(at=T0), msg(at=T0 + 5))) == ["word"])
    ok("a react on the message answers it", kinds(tx(msg(), use("mcp__cc-slack__react",
                                                               {"chat_id": CHAT, "ts": ROOT, "emoji": "+1"}))) == [])
    ok("a file sent into the thread answers it", kinds(tx(msg(), use("mcp__cc-slack__file",
                                                                     {"chat_id": CHAT, "path": "/tmp/x.png",
                                                                      "thread_ts": ROOT}))) == [])
    ok("`cc-slack post` from a script counts as a word",
       kinds(tx(msg(), use("Bash", {"command": f"cc-slack post {CHAT} --thread {ROOT} 'done'"}))) == [])
    ok("a word in another thread of the same channel does not answer this one",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep(root="1700000009.000900", ts="1700000009.000900"))) == ["word"])
    ok("two unanswered threads are two debts",
       sorted(d["root"] for d in debts(tx(msg(), msg(root="1700000009.000900", ts="1700000009.000900")))) ==
       [ROOT, "1700000009.000900"])
    ok("an injected local message has no Slack thread to owe", kinds(tx(msg(chat="local"))) == [])
    ok("a deletion notice settles what that message owed — it says disregard it",
       kinds(tx(msg(), msg("[the owner deleted their 23:43 message — disregard it]", at=T0 + 9))) == [])
    ok("an edit notice is the message again, and still owes a word",
       kinds(tx(msg(), msg("[the owner edited their 23:43 message; new text:] do the other thing", at=T0 + 9)))
       == ["word"])
    ok("a notice about a message they have since replaced does not settle the newer one",
       kinds(tx(msg(), msg("later", ts="1700000050.000500", at=T0 + 50),
                msg("[the owner deleted their 23:43 message — disregard it]", at=T0 + 60))) == ["word"])
    ok("their 👍 on our reply is an answer, not a question",
       kinds(tx(msg('👍 on your reply: "done"', ts="1700000020.000200", kind="reaction"))) == [])
    ok("...and it does not bury an older message they are still waiting on",
       kinds(tx(msg(at=T0 + 1), msg('👍 on your reply: "ok"', ts="1700000020.000200", kind="reaction",
                                    at=T0 + 20))) == ["word"])
    ok("a message from another source is not ours to answer",
       kinds(tx({"type": "user", "timestamp": iso(T0), "message": {"role": "user", "content": "<channel "
                 'source="other" chat_id="C9" thread_ts="1.0" ts="1.0">hi</channel>'}})) == [])

    # --- the cancels, read out of cc-slack's own files
    ok("a 🏁 after their message closes the thread",
       kinds(tx(msg()), flags={f"{CHAT}:{ROOT}": float(ROOT) + 1}, marks={}) == [])
    ok("a 🏁 older than their message does not",
       kinds(tx(msg()), flags={f"{CHAT}:{ROOT}": float(ROOT) - 1}, marks={}) == ["word"])
    ok("a ✅ newer than their message: the session called the thread done",
       kinds(tx(msg()), marks={f"{CHAT}:{ROOT}:white_check_mark": float(ROOT) + 1}, flags={}) == [])
    ok("a ❓ declared within the grace puts the ball in their court",
       kinds(tx(msg()), marks={f"{CHAT}:{ROOT}:question": float(ROOT)}, flags={}) == [])
    ok("a stale ❓ does not — their newer word re-owes it",
       kinds(tx(msg()), marks={f"{CHAT}:{ROOT}:question": float(ROOT) - GRACE - 1}, flags={}) == ["word"])

    # --- the second debt: our own question, on nobody's list
    ok("our question with no needs_owner is unlabelled",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("shall i deploy it?"))) == ["label"])
    ok("needs_owner IS the declaration", kinds(tx(msg(), rep("shall i deploy it?", needs=True))) == [])
    ok("their answer after our question ends it",
       kinds(tx(msg(at=T0), rep("shall i deploy it?", at=T0 + 2), msg("yes", ts="1700000030.000300", at=T0 + 30)))
       == ["word"])
    ok("a ❓ recorded for that thread is the same declaration",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("shall i deploy it?")), marks={f"{CHAT}:{ROOT}:question": T0 + 2}, flags={}) == [])
    ok("a question that is not the last word is not an ask",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("why? because it was faster. done."))) == [])
    ok("a question inside a code block is not an ask",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("ran it:\n```\necho ok?\n```"))) == [])
    ok("quoting their question back is not asking one",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("> is it done?"))) == [])
    ok("the box's own two forms declare themselves and are not this hook's business",
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("🔐 may I run this?"))) == [] and
       kinds(tx(msg(), rep("STATUS: BLOCKED: which one?"))) == [])

    # --- the Stop decision: once, silent when held, and it never writes cc-slack's files
    before = sorted(os.listdir(SLACK_DIR))
    t = tx(msg())
    pay = {"session_id": "sess-1", "transcript_path": t, "cwd": tmp}
    d1 = check(dict(pay))
    ok("the hook says what is owed, with the call that pays it",
       bool(d1) and "OWED" in d1 and f'chat_id="{CHAT}"' in d1 and "#one" in d1)
    ok("it is said once, not every turn", check(dict(pay)) is None)
    ok("never while another Stop hook is holding the session",
       check(dict(pay, stop_hook_active=True, session_id="sess-2")) is None)
    ok("a new debt in the same session is still said",
       bool(check({"session_id": "sess-1", "transcript_path": tx(msg(), msg(ts="1700000040.000400", at=T0 + 40)),
                   "cwd": tmp})))
    ok("nothing owed says nothing at all", check({"session_id": "sess-3", "transcript_path": tx(msg(), rep()),
                                                  "cwd": tmp}) is None)
    ok("a payload with no transcript is silent, not an error",
       check({"session_id": "nope", "transcript_path": "/nonexistent"}) is None and check({}) is None)
    with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "marks.json"), "w") as fh:
        fh.write("{not json at all")                   # cc-slack half-way through its own atomic write, or worse
    with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "flags.json"), "w") as fh:
        fh.write("")
    ok("a malformed mark file cancels nothing and breaks nothing",
       kinds(tx(msg())) == ["word"] and load_slack("marks.json") == {})
    os.remove(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "marks.json")); os.remove(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "flags.json"))
    half = tx(msg())
    with open(half, "a") as fh:
        fh.write('{"type":"user","message":{"content":"<channel sourc')     # a line still being written
    ok("a half-written transcript line is skipped, not fatal", kinds(half) == ["word"])
    ok("the age of the debt is said, and its first words",
       "just now" in told(debts(tx(msg(ts=str(time.time()), root=str(time.time()))))[0]))
    ok("cc-slack's own files are only ever read", sorted(os.listdir(SLACK_DIR)) == before)
    ok("every debt said is one countable line on the ledger", len(ledger_rows()) == 2)
    ok("the ledger says which debt, and whose session",
       ledger_rows()[0]["kind"] == "word" and ledger_rows()[0]["session_id"] == "sess-1")

    # --- THE ONE THREAD READ, on the real evidence of 2026-09-01: the finding said the owner's 23:56Z question
    # about developed/backlog items was unanswered and he was waiting. The answer went out 70 s later, in its thread.
    global BOARDS
    BOARDS = os.path.join(tmp, "boards")
    os.makedirs(BOARDS, exist_ok=True)
    ROOT, C1 = "1788306960.000100", "C0ALPHA111"
    ASKED = "2026-09-01T23:56:00Z"
    json.dump({"alpha": C1, "alpha-updates": "C0ALPHA222", "alpha--t9": "C0ALPHAT9R", "alerts": "C0ALERTS11",
               "approvals": "C0APPROV11"}, open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "channels.json"), "w"))
    with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), "w") as fh:
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:57:09Z\tpost\t{C1}\t{ROOT}\tat a high level, here is what is developed…\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-02T00:00:11Z\tpost\t{C1}\t{ROOT}\t…and here is the backlog\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:55:00Z\tpost\t{C1}\t1788306000.111111\ta word in ANOTHER thread, before the ask\n")
    idx = outbox_index()
    a = answered("#alpha", ROOT, epoch(ASKED), idx)
    ok("the 23:56Z question WAS answered: two words of ours in its thread, the first 70 s later",
       a["n"] == 2 and a["first"] == epoch("2026-09-01T23:57:09Z"))
    ok("a thread with nothing of ours in it is unanswered — the finding that is real",
       answered("#alpha", "1788306000.999999", epoch(ASKED), idx)["n"] == 0)
    ok("a word in another thread of the same channel does not answer this ask",
       answered("#alpha", ROOT, epoch("2026-09-02T00:01:00Z"), idx)["n"] == 0
       and answered("#alpha", "1788306000.111111", epoch(ASKED), idx)["n"] == 0)
    ok("a channel name resolves through cc-slack's channels.json, and an id is itself",
       chat_ref("#alpha") == (C1, True) and chat_ref(C1) == (C1, True) and chat_id("#alpha") == C1)
    # PR #194 finding 1: cc-scope writes `slack dm <ts>` for an IM and `dm` is no channel's name, so the old
    # reader scored every answered DM n=0 — "unanswered", the one state the rubric flags. An unresolved name
    # now says so, and the thread is still found by its root, which is a microsecond stamp.
    ok("a name the box cannot resolve says so — and the DM's own thread is still found by its root",
       chat_ref("dm")[1] is False and chat_ref("dm")[0] == "dm"
       and answered("dm", ROOT, epoch(ASKED), idx)["n"] == 2
       and answered("dm", ROOT, epoch(ASKED), idx)["chat_unknown"] is True)
    ok("…and when the root is nowhere either, it is chat_unknown, never a bare n=0 that reads as 'nobody answered'",
       answered("dm", "1788306000.999999", epoch(ASKED), idx)["chat_unknown"] is True
       and answered("#alpha", "1788306000.999999", epoch(ASKED), idx)["chat_unknown"] is False)
    ok("second resolution, and the same second counts: outbox.log prints seconds",
       answered("#alpha", ROOT, epoch("2026-09-01T23:57:09Z"), idx)["n"] == 2
       and answered("#alpha", ROOT, epoch("2026-09-01T23:57:10Z"), idx)["n"] == 1)
    got = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--answered"], input=f"#alpha\t{ROOT}\t{ASKED}\n"
                         f"#alpha\t1788306000.999999\t{ASKED}\n", capture_output=True, text=True,
                         env=dict(os.environ, CC_SLACK_DIR=SLACK_DIR)).stdout.splitlines()
    ok("`--answered` says the same to cc-secretary, one JSON object a line, each echoing the query it answers",
       len(got) == 2 and json.loads(got[0])["n"] == 2 and json.loads(got[0])["first"] == "2026-09-01T23:57:09Z"
       and json.loads(got[0])["since"] == ASKED and json.loads(got[0])["chat_unknown"] is False
       and json.loads(got[0])["unseen"] is False
       and json.loads(got[1])["n"] == 0 and json.loads(got[1])["first"] == "")

    # PR #194 finding 2: a REACTION is the answer whenever yes/no is the whole of it — cc-slack's react tool says
    # so to sessions, and the transcript half above has always counted one. cc-slack now logs an added reaction to
    # this same outbox, so both halves see it; its bookkeeping ones (👀 received, the ❓🔴🟠 marks, 🏁) answer nothing.
    RXA, RXB = "1788306100.100000", "1788306100.200000"
    with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), "a") as fh:
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:00Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXA}\t+1\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:00Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXB}\teyes\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:01Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXB}\tred_circle\n")
    rx = outbox_index()
    ok("a 👍 on their message answers it — the same read, so the two halves cannot disagree about a react",
       answered("#alpha", RXA, epoch(ASKED), rx)["n"] == 1)
    ok("…and a 👀 or a bot mark answers nothing: that thread is still unanswered",
       answered("#alpha", RXB, epoch(ASKED), rx)["n"] == 0)
    with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), "a") as fh:
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:59:00Z\treact-auto\t{C1}\t{RXB}\t+1\n")
    ok("the box's OWN 👍 for a session that said nothing is not an answer — that is the silence to notice",
       answered("#alpha", RXB, epoch(ASKED), outbox_index())["n"] == 0)
    # review of PR #194, finding 1: a session may react with the NAME or with the character, and cc-slack writes
    # down whichever it was handed — so a 👀 spelt 👀 read as a word, and a question nobody answered became
    # unflaggable: `answered=yes` on the ask, and the owner debt reading a thread he only ever got a 👀 in as reached.
    RXC, RXD = "1788306100.300000", "1788306100.400000"
    with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), "a") as fh:
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:02Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXC}\t👀\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:03Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXC}\t:eyes:\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:04Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXC}\t🏁\n")
        fh.write(f"2026-09-01T23:58:05Z\treact\t{C1}\t{RXD}\t👍\n")
    rxc = outbox_index()
    ok("a 👀 spelt as the character, or in colons, is the same 'received' as `eyes` — none of them answers anything",
       answered("#alpha", RXC, epoch(ASKED), rxc)["n"] == 0)
    ok("…and a 👍 spelt as the character is still the answer it is: only the marks are dropped, not every character",
       answered("#alpha", RXD, epoch(ASKED), rxc)["n"] == 1)

    # --- the owner debt: the board's own `waiting`, and whether he was reached since
    def wrote_board(status, updated, repo="alpha", track="t9", log=None, pr=""):
        json.dump({"repo": repo, "tracks": {track: {"status": status, "title": "the push refuses",
                                                    "updated": updated, "pr": pr}}, "log": log or []},
                  open(os.path.join(BOARDS, f"{repo}.json"), "w"))

    def said(at, chat, text, thread=""):
        with open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), "a") as fh:
            fh.write(f"{at}\tpost\t{chat}\t{thread}\t{text}\n")

    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T01:00:00Z")
    d = owner_debt("alpha", "t9")
    ok("the board says waiting and nothing has gone to him since: that is the debt",
       d and d["kind"] == "owner" and d["chat"] == C1)
    ok("it says the row, and how to reach him", "alpha/t9" in told(d) and "needs_owner=true" in told(d))
    ok("a session idle at an empty prompt raises nothing — its row is running, not waiting",
       (wrote_board("running", "2026-09-02T01:00:00Z"), owner_debt("alpha", "t9"))[1] is None)
    ok("a session that answered a terminal question in the terminal raises nothing, for the same reason",
       (wrote_board("done", "2026-09-02T01:00:00Z"), owner_debt("alpha", "t9"))[1] is None)
    ok("no board row at all raises nothing",
       owner_debt("alpha", "nosuchtrack") is None and owner_debt("nosuchrepo", "t9") is None)
    said("2026-09-02T01:02:00Z", "C0ALPHA222", "alpha/t9 is blocked on you")
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T01:00:00Z")
    ok("the debt clears when he is reached by ANY rung — the updates lane counts", owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    waited = [{"ts": "2026-09-02T01:00:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}]
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T01:03:00Z",
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T01:03:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    ok("…and a word sent BEFORE it entered waiting does not clear it", owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    # PR #194 finding 3: cc-board bumps `updated` on every note and set, so dating the wait from it re-fired a
    # debt the session had already paid — told at 01:02, a note at 01:05, and the hook said "HE DOES NOT KNOW".
    # The wait starts where the board's own log says it started.
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T01:05:00Z",
                log=waited + [{"ts": "2026-09-02T01:04:00Z", "track": "other", "msg": "status -> waiting"},
                              {"ts": "2026-09-02T01:05:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "a note somebody typed"}])
    ok("a note after he was told does not re-open the debt: `updated` moves, the wait does not",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    ok("…and another track's log lines are not this one's wait",
       wait_start(board("alpha"), "t9", board_row(board("alpha"), "t9")) == epoch("2026-09-02T01:00:00Z"))
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T01:06:00Z",
                log=waited + [{"ts": "2026-09-02T01:04:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> running"},
                              {"ts": "2026-09-02T01:06:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    ok("…but going back INTO waiting after he was told is a new wait, and that does raise",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    # and his own lane has to be nameable: cc-slack unlinks channels.json on mkchannel, and in that window every
    # ref reads n=0 — calling that "he was never told" is this same false alarm from the other end.
    os.rename(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "channels.json"), os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "channels.kept"))
    _CHANS.clear()
    ok("with channels.json away, no owner debt: we cannot see the lane he reads, so we do not claim he is unread",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    os.rename(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "channels.kept"), os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "channels.json"))
    _CHANS.clear()
    ok("…and with it back, the same board raises it again", owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)

    # --- THE LANES, and what a word in one has to say to be about THIS track. The review of PR #194 found this
    # one omission pointing both ways: #approvals was not a lane at all, so the box's own way of putting a
    # decision in front of him did not pay the debt (finding 2) — and the lanes that were read were read whole,
    # so any post about anything paid every waiting track's debt at once (finding 3).
    PR7 = "https://github.com/o/alpha/pull/7"
    entered = [{"ts": "2026-09-02T02:00:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}]
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T02:00:00Z", log=entered, pr=PR7)
    ok("a fresh wait with nothing said about it since is the debt", owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    said("2026-09-02T02:01:00Z", "C0ALERTS11", "*alpha/t2 done* Track finished after 1 iteration(s)")
    said("2026-09-02T02:01:30Z", C1, "PR #9 landed — that was the other track")
    ok("the box's news about OTHER tracks does not pay this one's debt: read whole, #alerts' 122 posts about "
       "everything else made all three of that day's waiting rows read as reached",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    said("2026-09-02T02:02:00Z", "C0APPROV11", f"[alpha] PR #7: the push refuses — {PR7}")
    ok("a card in #approvals IS the decision put in front of him — it names the row's own PR, and that is paid",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T03:00:00Z", pr=PR7,
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T03:00:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    ok("…and the next wait starts unpaid: the card was posted before it", owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    said("2026-09-02T03:01:00Z", "C0ALERTS11", "*alpha/t9* the push still refuses — over to you")
    ok("a word in a shared lane that NAMES the track pays it, whichever lane it is",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T04:00:00Z", pr=PR7,
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T04:00:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    ok("…and that one is paid too, so the next wait after it starts unpaid again",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    said("2026-09-02T04:01:00Z", "C0ALPHAT9R", "the push still refuses — over to you")
    ok("the track's OWN channel needs no naming: everything in #alpha--t9 is about t9",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    # review of PR #194, finding 2: what the debt TELLS the session to do has to be what it counts. #alpha is his
    # own lane and carries every track in the repo, so a word there that names no row pays nothing — and a session
    # that did exactly as it was told would be told the same thing again, about a message he already has.
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T05:30:00Z", pr=PR7,
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T05:30:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    d5 = owner_debt("alpha", "t9")
    said("2026-09-02T05:31:00Z", C1, "the push still refuses — over to you")
    ok("a word in HIS OWN lane that names no row does not pay it either: #alpha carries every track in the repo",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    ok("…and the debt says so — it asks for the row's name, or its PR, in the word it prescribes",
       "alpha/t9" in told(d5) and PR7 in told(d5) and "needs_owner=true" in told(d5))
    said("2026-09-02T05:32:00Z", C1, "alpha/t9 — the push still refuses, over to you")
    ok("…and doing what it says pays it: the prescribed word names the row, so the rule counts it",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)

    # review of PR #194, finding 1: the bare track name was matched as a plain substring, so a routine line about
    # a DIFFERENT row cleared this debt and he was never told. Both ends are bounded now, which also stops a
    # LONGER row's name paying a shorter one's. Each half asserts the case kept as well as the one suppressed.
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T06:00:00Z", track="api",
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T06:00:00Z", "track": "api", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    said("2026-09-02T06:01:00Z", "C0ALERTS11", "*alpha/rapid-fix done* Track finished after 1 iteration(s)")
    ok("a shared-lane post carrying the track name inside another word leaves the debt standing",
       owner_debt("alpha", "api") is not None)
    said("2026-09-02T06:02:00Z", "C0ALERTS11", "*alpha/api* the push still refuses — over to you")
    ok("…while the same lane naming the row itself still pays it", owner_debt("alpha", "api") is None)
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T07:00:00Z", track="t9",
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T07:00:00Z", "track": "t9", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    said("2026-09-02T07:01:00Z", "C0ALERTS11", "*alpha/t9x done* Track finished after 1 iteration(s)")
    ok("…and a longer row's name does not pay a shorter one's: alpha/t9 is bounded at its tail too",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)

    # --- both halves off the ONE read: ONE line pays the session's debt here and answers his question there, so
    # the two can never disagree about a thread. (The debt is about a track, so the word has to say which one.)
    wrote_board("waiting", ASKED, track="t8", log=[{"ts": ASKED, "track": "t8", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    ok("with nothing said about t8, the debt stands — an answer in another track's thread is not this one's",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t8") is not None)
    said("2026-09-02T05:00:00Z", C1, "alpha/t8 — answered, in the thread he asked it in", thread=ROOT)
    ok("a question answered in its thread is answered for BOTH halves — no finding for cc-secretary, no debt here",
       answered("#alpha", ROOT, epoch(ASKED))["n"] == 3 and owner_debt("alpha", "t8") is None)
    # a read that FAILED must not come back as the alarm. An outbox that will not open (a directory here, so the
    # test holds whoever runs it) is not "nobody answered": --answered says nothing, and no owner debt is raised.
    os.rename(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.kept"))
    os.mkdir(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"))
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T01:03:00Z")
    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--answered"], input=f"#alpha\t{ROOT}\t{ASKED}\n",
                       capture_output=True, text=True, env=dict(os.environ, CC_SLACK_DIR=SLACK_DIR))
    ok("an outbox that will not read says NOTHING, which cc-secretary reads as unknown — never as unanswered",
       r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() == "")
    ok("…and it raises no owner debt either: a failed read never claims he was not told",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is None)
    os.rmdir(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"))
    ok("an outbox that is simply ABSENT is an honest empty, not a failure: the debt stands",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t9") is not None)
    os.rename(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.kept"), os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"))

    # review of PR #194, 3rd pass: `outbox_index` reads the last OUTBOX_TAIL of the log, but the debt above asks an
    # UNBOUNDED question — has anything reached him since the row entered waiting. Once the log rolls past the word
    # that paid it, that word is invisible, and the n=0 it leaves behind reads exactly like silence: the hook nags
    # him about a message he already has. (This change makes the log roll faster — it adds a line per reaction.)
    PAID = "2026-09-02T08:00:00Z"
    wrote_board("waiting", PAID, track="t7", pr=PR7, log=[{"ts": PAID, "track": "t7", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    open(os.path.join(SLACK_DIR, "outbox.log"), "w").close()          # this case builds its own log, first line up
    said("2026-09-02T08:01:00Z", C1, "alpha/t7 — the push refuses, over to you")     # he WAS told, right at the top
    for i in range(60):
        said(f"2026-09-02T09:{i:02d}:00Z", "C0ALERTS11", "another repo's news, " + "x" * 200)   # …then 15 kB of log
    rolled = outbox_index(nbytes=2048)
    ok("a word that has rolled off the tail of the log is not silence: no debt out of a span the read cannot see",
       rolled.begins > epoch("2026-09-02T08:01:00Z") and owner_debt("alpha", "t7", rolled) is None)
    ok("…and `answered` says which it is: the old span is unseen, one inside the tail is not, and a read that took "
       "the whole file hides nothing from anybody",
       answered("#alpha", None, epoch(PAID), rolled)["unseen"] is True
       and answered("#alpha", None, epoch("2026-09-02T10:00:00Z"), rolled)["unseen"] is False
       and answered("#alpha", None, epoch(PAID), outbox_index())["unseen"] is False)
    wrote_board("waiting", "2026-09-02T10:00:00Z", track="t7", pr=PR7,
                log=[{"ts": "2026-09-02T10:00:00Z", "track": "t7", "msg": "status -> waiting"}])
    ok("…but a wait that began inside what the tail CAN see is still judged: only the rolled-off part is excused",
       owner_debt("alpha", "t7", rolled) is not None)
    ok("…and the same log read whole still finds the word that paid the first wait: nothing present is muted",
       answered("#alpha", None, epoch(PAID), outbox_index(), ["alpha/t7"])["n"] == 1)

    ok("the two debts of before are untouched by any of it",
       kinds(tx(msg())) == ["word"] and kinds(tx(msg(), rep())) == []
       and kinds(tx(msg(), rep()), where=("alpha", "nosuchtrack")) == [])

    # --- --with: this hook carries the other one's decision, ours first
    ctx = json.dumps({"decision": "block", "reason": "CONTEXT 42%. Journal now."})
    m = json.loads(merge("OWED — answer them.", ctx))
    ok("both hooks say their piece in one decision, what is owed first",
       m["decision"] == "block" and m["reason"].startswith("OWED") and "CONTEXT 42%" in m["reason"])
    ok("nothing owed passes the other hook's decision through untouched",
       json.loads(merge(None, ctx))["reason"] == "CONTEXT 42%. Journal now.")
    ok("nothing at all from either hook says nothing", merge(None, None) == "" and merge(None, "") == "")

    import shutil
    shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    print(f"cc-owed selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
